      "id": 1,
      "text": "Dresbach, Minnesota, was named after its founder, George B. Dresbach, who settled the area around 1855–1857.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/dresbach_minnesota"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "A station on the Chicago, Milwaukee and St. Paul Railroad opened in Dresbach by the late 1850s, linking it to regional markets.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/dresbach_township_winona_county_minnesota"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Mississippi River runs along Dresbach’s western edge, and Interstate 90 cuts through the town, connecting Winona and La Crosse.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        29,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dresbach%2C_Minnesota"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Dresbach Bridge, opened in 1965 and replaced in 2016, spans the Mississippi and carries I‑90 traffic.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/dresbach_township_winona_county_minnesota"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "In its heyday, Dresbach hosted a sawmill, brickworks, and limestone quarry, fueling regional construction.",
